; ============================================================================
; DOES derive_distinct_alias() UNIQUIFY A NAME THAT IS ONLY TAKEN ELSEWHERE?
; ============================================================================
;
; A PROBE, NOT A REGISTERED SPEC. It is not in kRegressionSpecs and registering
; it would be an engine edit; it runs by DOTSCRIPT with SET ALTERNATE capture.
; If it confirms, it becomes a candidate arm and that promotion is its own act.
;
; WHAT IT MEASURES. R129 sec 8 bullet 1, which is explicitly REASONED AND NOT
; RUN -- the ruling says so in its own opening line: "Line-cited claims are
; MEASURED; the consequences are reasoned, not run." The claim:
;
;   derive_distinct_alias() (src/cli/cmd_use.cpp:491, called at :917) renames
;   STUDENTS to STUDENTS2 when the name is taken, and asks
;   find_open_area_by_alias() -- the workspace-blind one -- whether it is
;   taken. Under a scoped cursor the name is NOT taken (it is taken in another
;   workspace), so this uniquifies where it should not.
;
; MEASURED IN SOURCE 2026-08-31, four days after the ruling: unchanged.
; derive_distinct_alias() still loops n = 2..999 asking
; find_open_area_by_alias(cand, except_slot), and that resolver carries no
; workspace filter. So the precondition holds; what is NOT known is the
; BEHAVIOUR, and that is the whole point of running it.
;
; WHY IT IS WORTH RUNNING RATHER THAN REASONING FURTHER. AIF-137 was predicted
; in writing on 2026-08-23 and not measured until 2026-08-27, and when it was
; run it fired on the first attempt with no tuning. A reasoned consequence and
; a measured one are different evidence tiers in this house. This file moves
; one bullet from the lower tier to the higher one, in whichever direction the
; engine actually goes.
;
; PREDICTIONS, WRITTEN BEFORE THE RUN so the result cannot be read backwards:
;
;   P1 expected .T.  -- the second USE lands as DDAT2, because the resolver it
;                       consults is workspace-blind and A's copy is open.
;   P2 expected to PRINT NOTHING -- if the alias was taken, SELECT DDAT inside
;                       workspace B resolves to A's copy, whose label is
;                       A_ONE, so the equality reads .F. rather than erroring.
;
;   If P1 prints .F. or does not print at all AND P2 reads .T., the ruling's
;   bullet is FALSIFIED and R129 sec 8 wants a correction, which is an equally
;   good outcome and is why both directions are armed.
;
; HOW IT ASSERTS. By FIELD VALUE, never by console text. The two directories
; hold the SAME table name with DIFFERENT labels, so a label read says which
; copy answered. Ground truth is taken BY SLOT before any name is tested,
; because RELWSNAME's header states the rule this file obeys: "ADDRESSING IS BY
; ENGINE SLOT, NOT BY NAME, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING" -- a name-addressed guard
; could not tell you which table it read, in a file about name resolution.
;
; COUNT DISCIPLINE. Markers are DDA_G0a, DDA_G0b, DDA_G1a, DDA_G1b, then DDA_P1
; and DDA_P2. That is SIX lines expected. An errored marker PRINTS NOTHING
; rather than going red, so a SHORT COUNT IS A RESULT, not a clean run -- and
; for P1/P2 specifically, one of them printing nothing IS the finding. Count
; the DDA_ lines first and read the verdicts second.
;
; GUARDS. G0a/G0b prove each fixture on its own before any workspace exists.
; G1a/G1b prove the two copies landed at the slots the probe reasons about.
; IF ANY GUARD READS .F., TREAT P1 AND P2 AS UNPROVEN RATHER THAN AS ANSWERS.
;
; SET PATH ORDERING: every CREATE and every ERASE below is immediately preceded
; by the SET PATH that owns it. workspace_additive_open.dts was once edited so
; a SET PATH landed BEFORE the previous table's CREATE and a fixture was built
; in the wrong directory.
;
; Self-bootstrapping in DBF/SANDBOX/DDAA and DBF/SANDBOX/DDAB, erased at the
; end; both workspaces destroyed in teardown (D10.3) so the NAMES are freed and
; a later run mints fresh rather than adopting. Catalog rows remain as history.
;
; ============================================================================

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL

; --- fixtures: ONE name, TWO directories, different labels ------------------

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/DDAA
SELECT 0
CREATE X64 DDAT (DK C(4), DLBL C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE DK WITH "1"
REPLACE DLBL WITH "A_ONE"

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/DDAB
SELECT 1
CREATE X64 DDAT (DK C(4), DLBL C(10))
APPEND
REPLACE DK WITH "1"
REPLACE DLBL WITH "B_ONE"

SELECT 0
GO 1
? "DDA_G0a_A_fixture_written:" + (ALLTRIM(DLBL) = "A_ONE")
SELECT 1
GO 1
? "DDA_G0b_B_fixture_written:" + (ALLTRIM(DLBL) = "B_ONE")

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL

; --- two workspaces, the SAME table name opened by USE in each --------------
; USE is the path derive_distinct_alias() sits on. WORKSPACE OPEN <dir> takes a
; different route, which is why this probe cannot borrow RELWSNAME's fixture.

WORKSPACE NEW DDAWSA
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/DDAA
SELECT 0
USE DDAT

WORKSPACE NEW DDAWSB
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/DDAB
SELECT 1
USE DDAT

; Ground truth BY SLOT, before any name is tested.
SELECT 0
GO 1
? "DDA_G1a_slot0_is_workspace_A_copy:" + (ALLTRIM(DLBL) = "A_ONE")
SELECT 1
GO 1
? "DDA_G1b_slot1_is_workspace_B_copy:" + (ALLTRIM(DLBL) = "B_ONE")

; --- the probe --------------------------------------------------------------
; P1: if the alias was uniquified, DDAT2 exists and it is B's copy.
; P2: if it was NOT uniquified, the plain name inside B is B's copy.
; Exactly one of these should carry the answer; the other prints nothing or
; reads .F. Both are positive reads of a field.

SELECT DDAT2
? "DDA_P1_second_use_was_uniquified:" + (ALLTRIM(DLBL) = "B_ONE")

SELECT DDAT
? "DDA_P2_plain_name_inside_B_is_B:" + (ALLTRIM(DLBL) = "B_ONE")

; --- teardown ---------------------------------------------------------------

WORKSPACE CLOSE ALL
WORKSPACE DESTROY DDAWSA
WORKSPACE DESTROY DDAWSB

SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/DDAA
ERASE DDAT CONFIRM
SET PATH DBF DBF/SANDBOX/DDAB
ERASE DDAT CONFIRM
